From 68e8b2a28b685ddde38ed225ad595f0b4f1004f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex <richardstallman42@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:50:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] fix(threads): size smash thread stacks for the
 application's static TLS

On an aarch64 host smash printed "pthread_create(coordinator) failed;
compression disabled". The coordinator and helpers were created with a
fixed 128 KiB stack, and glibc places the application's static TLS block
and the guard page inside each thread's stack; once an application carries
~128 KiB of static TLS (large C++ and ML stacks do) every one of those
pthread_create calls fails with EINVAL at library load, and smash runs as a
plain allocator for the whole process. Not architecture-specific: on x86-64
100 KiB of static TLS worked and 128/256 KiB reproduced the message exactly.
A 64 KiB-page kernel makes it worse (the guard alone is 64 KiB).

util/thread_stack.h sizes a smash thread's stack as its own need plus
glibc's minimum (__pthread_get_minstack, the GLIBC_PRIVATE symbol Chromium
uses for the same purpose), rounded to the runtime page size, and retries
with default attributes if still refused. Used for the helpers, the
coordinator and the NUMA node workers, whose 1 MiB stacks and 4 KiB guard
(compile-time kPageSize, which a 64 KiB-page kernel rejects) had the same
flaw. The coordinator warning now carries the error, and a short helper
pool is reported instead of silently truncated.

Verified: with 128 KiB, 256 KiB, 1 MiB and 4 MiB of static TLS there is no
warning and compression runs as with none. New
test_static_tls_threads_{full,large_only}: 512 KiB of static TLS, and a
cold compressible 16 MiB block must leave RSS; 0% resident with the fix,
100% (FAIL) with the previous library. Suite 52/52.

The std::bad_alloc reported alongside the warning is NOT explained by this:
with compression disabled the test applications still allocate normally.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
